course: euphemisms
lesson: 40
title: Soft words for hard things: rudeness and swearing
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: The rudest words are not always swear words. Grown-ups often say rude things in very polite clothes, and the polite words are a warning sign.
explain: "With all due respect" sounds respectful, but it nearly always comes just before "you are wrong". "No offence, but" comes just before something offensive.
explain: Office emails have their own polite rudeness: "per my last email" means "you did not read what I already wrote", and news reports call a shouting match "a frank exchange of views".
explain: There are also rude gestures with jokey names, like "the one-finger salute", which is no salute at all but an insult made with the middle finger.
explain: To understand polite rudeness, ignore the kind words and listen for what comes after them and how it is said.
fact: "Go take a running jump" means go away; it is a British way of telling someone off.
fact: "The one-finger salute" is a jokey name for raising the middle finger as an insult.
fact: "Flip the bird" means raise the middle finger as an insult; it is American slang.
fact: "The two-fingered salute" is a jokey British name for the rude V-sign made with two fingers, palm turned inwards.
fact: "Shut the front door!" is an American jokey cry of surprise, a cleaned-up form of a ruder phrase; it has nothing to do with doors.
fact: "With all due respect" usually means "I disagree with you, or I think you are wrong".
fact: "With the greatest respect" usually means "I think you are quite wrong"; in British speech it can mean "you are a fool".
fact: "I hear what you're saying" often means "I disagree and I will not change my mind"; it is British and polite.
fact: "Bless your heart" in the American South can mean "you poor foolish thing", hiding pity or scorn behind sweet words.
fact: "Not to be rude, but" warns that what comes next is rude.
fact: "No offence, but" warns that what comes next will offend.
fact: "Per my last email" means "you did not read my email properly"; it is office jargon.
fact: "As previously mentioned" means "I already told you this"; it is office jargon.
fact: "Let's agree to disagree" means "let's stop arguing, because I will not change my mind".
fact: "I'll bear it in mind" often means "I will ignore what you just said"; it is polite British speech.
fact: "A frank exchange of views" means an angry argument; it is the language of officials and news reports.
fact: "A robust discussion" means a heated argument; it is official language.
fact: "Not a people person" means unfriendly or rude to others; it is said kindly.
fact: "Interesting choice" said about clothes, food or a decision usually means "a bad choice".
fact: "A word in your ear" means a quiet telling-off in private; it is British.
exercise: What does "go take a running jump" mean? | Go away | Go and do sport | Go and win a race | Go and get fit
exercise: What is "the one-finger salute"? | Raising the middle finger as an insult | A soldier's polite greeting to an officer on parade | Pointing the way to someone | Waving goodbye to a friend
exercise: What does "flip the bird" mean? | Raise the middle finger as an insult | Turn a chicken over in the pan as it cooks | Throw a bird into the air | Copy a bird's dance
exercise: In Britain, what is "the two-fingered salute"? | The rude V-sign | A scout's greeting | A peace sign | A wave to the King
exercise: "Shut the front door! You won the lottery?" What is the speaker doing? | Showing surprise | Asking you to close a door | Being angry with you | Telling you to stop talking
exercise: In a meeting, "With all due respect, that plan won't work." What is the speaker really doing? | Disagreeing with the other person | Praising the other person's clever ideas | Agreeing politely | Asking for respect
exercise: What does "With the greatest respect, minister" usually mean in Britain? | I think you are quite wrong | I admire you very much | I respect you most of all | I am ready to obey you
exercise: The manager says, "I hear what you're saying." What does he usually mean? | I disagree and won't change my mind | I agree completely with you and will act on it | I can hear you well now | Please speak a bit louder
exercise: A Southern US lady says "Well, bless your heart" after you lock yourself out. What does she often mean? | You poor foolish thing | God will reward you | You have a good heart | You need a doctor
exercise: What usually comes after "Not to be rude, but"? | Something rude | A big compliment | A thank-you | A question about the time
exercise: What usually comes after "No offence, but"? | Something that will offend | Something very kind about the listener | An apology | Good news
exercise: An email starts "Per my last email". What is the writer really saying? | You did not read my email properly | This is my final email ever, I am leaving the job | My last email was lost | Please email me back
exercise: What does "as previously mentioned" really say? | I already told you this | I forgot what I said | This is brand new | Nobody told you this
exercise: What does "Let's agree to disagree" mean? | Let's stop arguing, I won't change my mind | Let's both agree now | Let's keep arguing all day until one of us wins | Let's ask a teacher
exercise: Mum suggests a tidy room; teen says "I'll bear it in mind." What does it often mean? | I will ignore it | I will do it now | I will remember it forever | I will think of a bear
exercise: The news says leaders had "a frank exchange of views". What happened? | They had an angry argument | They swapped gifts | They had a friendly chat | They met a man called Frank
exercise: What is "a robust discussion" in official language? | A heated argument | A strong friendship | A long lecture | A talk about robots
exercise: "He's not really a people person." What is meant? | He is unfriendly or rude to others | He prefers animals as pets to human friends | He works with computers | He lives far from town
exercise: Your aunt sees your orange-and-pink outfit and says "Interesting choice." What does she likely mean? | It is a bad choice | It is a clever choice | It is an exciting choice | It is a rare choice
exercise: The headteacher says, "A word in your ear, Tom." What is coming? | A quiet telling-off | A secret present | A spelling test | A hearing check
test: Headline: "Talks end after 'frank exchange of views' between the two leaders." What really happened? | The leaders argued angrily | The leaders became friends | The leaders swapped houses | The leaders agreed on everything
test: Office email: "As previously mentioned (twice), the report is due Friday." How does the writer feel? | Annoyed at having to repeat it | Happy to remind you again, as a friendly help | Unsure of the date | Sorry for being late
test: "no offence but ur drawing looks like a potato" Was the speaker kind? | No, it is an insult with a polite start | Yes, potatoes are lovely | Yes, "no offence" makes it kind | No, it is a joke about food
test: A footballer was sent off for flipping the bird at the crowd. What did he do? | He raised his middle finger at them | He threw a pigeon at them | He did a backflip in front of the fans to celebrate | He waved a flag at them
test: Sam says "with all due respect" means you really respect the person a lot. right? | No, it usually comes before "you're wrong" | Yes, it means great respect | Yes, it is the polite greeting used for a king or queen | No, it means you are scared
test: Council report: "The meeting saw a robust discussion of the parking plan." What happened at the meeting? | People argued heatedly about it | People ate a large meal | People agreed with no fuss at all, quickly and calmly | People built a car park
test: "Mate, shut the front door, you got a puppy??" What is the speaker feeling? | Surprised and excited | Cold from the open door | Angry about the puppy | Bored and tired
test: Teacher's note: "Dad, I'll bear your idea in mind." The idea is never mentioned again. What did "bear it in mind" mean? | The teacher was not going to use it | The teacher used it the next day | The teacher wrote it on the board | The teacher drew a bear
test: "piss off, go take a running jump" What does the speaker want? | The other person to go away | The other person to do the long jump | The other person to run faster | The other person to come back
test: A politician answers his rival: "I hear what the honourable member is saying..." What will he most likely do next? | Argue against him | Agree with everything | Admit he is wrong | Leave the room
test: Gossip: "His new boss is not exactly a people person — she shouted at the cleaner." What is she like? | Unfriendly and rude | Very kind and warm | Very shy and quiet | Good at making friends
test: "bless ur heart, u tried to microwave the ice cream to soften it?? lol" What does the speaker think? | That was a foolish thing to do | That was a clever idea | That was a kind thing to do | That was very brave
test: A boss and worker can't agree, and the boss says "Let's agree to disagree." What happens next? | The argument stops; neither changes their mind | They both agree with each other in the end after all | The worker wins the argument | The boss changes her mind
test: Which of these is a rude gesture, not rude words? | The two-fingered salute | Per my last email | No offence, but | Interesting choice
exam: Where an official communiqué records "a frank exchange of views", identify the nature of the encounter so described. | An acrimonious disagreement | A cordial and harmonious meeting | An exchange of diplomatic gifts | A meeting with a person named Frank
exam: Ascertain the meaning most accurately conveyed by the prefatory phrase "with all due respect". | The speaker is about to express disagreement | The speaker is about to express admiration | The speaker is about to tender an apology | The speaker is about to make a request
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established meaning of the expression "per my last email" in correspondence. | The recipient has failed to read prior correspondence attentively | The sender intends to cease all correspondence forthwith | The previous correspondence was lost in transmission | The sender requests a prompt reply by return
exam: Identify the gesture denoted by the expression "the one-finger salute". | The raising of the middle finger as an insult | A formal military greeting with the hand | The indication of a direction with the finger | A gesture requesting silence from others
